# dev-guardian

This project has the **dev-guardian MCP server** registered. 53 tools and
18 resources for security, quality, bugfix, deps, compliance,
observability, performance, plus first-class WordPress and .NET support.
All scanners run **locally**, no telemetry.

Prefer these MCP tools over invoking scanners directly. The MCP layer
persists results to `.guardian/guardian.db` for diffing, baselines, and
regression tracking.

## When the user asks for X → invoke tool Y

### Generic security
- "is this safe?" / "audit security" → `security_scan_full`
- "secrets leak?" → `scan_secrets`
- "vulnerable deps / CVEs?" → `scan_deps` or `deps_audit`
- "Dockerfile / container security" → `scan_containers`
- "Terraform / K8s / IaC" → `scan_iac`
- "deep bug hunt" → `bug_hunt`

### Quality / review
- "review before PR" → `review_pr`
- "code quality / duplication" → `quality_check`

### Deps
- "upgrade plan" → `deps_update_plan` (npm/pip/composer/cargo/go/bundler/dotnet)
- "audit deps + bot config" → `deps_audit`

### Compliance
- "GDPR / SOC2 / ISO27001" → `compliance_check` then `compliance_evidence`
- "SBOM" → `generate_sbom`
- "license compatibility" → `license_compatibility`

### Ops
- "bootstrap" → `init_project`
- "install scanners" → `install_toolchain`
- "detect stack" → `detect_stack`
- "logging / metrics" → `observability_setup`
- "performance / Lighthouse / k6" → `perf_check`

### WordPress
- "scan WP code" → `scan_wordpress`
- "audit live WP install" → `wp_audit`
- "any WP CVEs?" → `wp_vuln_check`
- "WP cron / backdoor check" → `wp_cron_audit`
- "WP REST API exposed?" → `wp_rest_audit`
- "WP posture overview" → `wp_describe_setup`
- "hardening checklist" → `wp_recommend_hardening`
- "check one plugin" → `wp_plugin_check`
- "audit N WP sites in parallel" → `bulk_audit_wordpress_sites`

### .NET / C#
- ".NET secrets in configs?" → `scan_dotnet_secrets`
- "EOL frameworks?" → `dotnet_target_framework_check`
- "EF Core migration safety" → `dotnet_efcore_audit`
- ".NET posture overview" → `dotnet_describe_setup`
- ".NET SAST" → `scan_sast` (auto includes p/csharp + SCS when opted in)

### Attack surface / DAST
- "what routes does this app expose?" → `map_attack_surface`
- "active DAST / pen-test the running app" → `map_attack_surface` first for
  the route inventory, then `scan_dast` against the already-running app
  (loopback-only unless `authorized_target: true`; a clean result is not
  evidence of injection safety — nuclei's opt-in pass covers the origin,
  not this project's specific routes)
- "is this finding reachable / exploitable?" → `map_attack_surface` then
  `validate_finding` (per-finding reachable/unreachable/unknown verdict with
  evidence, report-only; `unreachable` never available for Ruby/Java/C#/PHP —
  it IS produced, and can be wrong, for a CLI/cron/queue-only file or an
  unresolvable dynamic import)

### Meta
- "full audit" → `audit_executive` (stack-aware: auto includes WP/.NET when detected)
- "risk score" → `risk_score`
- "what's new since last scan?" → `diff_scans`
- "set baseline" → `set_baseline`
- "noise reduction" → `triage_findings`
- "prioritise findings" → `prioritize_findings`
- "how do I fix this?" → `suggest_fix`
- "export report" → `report_export`
- "GitHub issues from findings" → `create_github_issues` (local `gh`)
- "pre-commit hooks" → `precommit_install`
- "register custom Semgrep rules" → `register_custom_rules`
- "health check" → `health_status`
- "regression check" → `regression_alert`
- "SBOM diff" → `sbom_diff`
- "set up another AI host" → run `node cli/dev-guardian.mjs mcp-config <host>` (CLI)

## Resources (`guardian://…`)

Findings + scans: `findings/open`, `findings/critical`,
`findings/by-severity/{level}` (paginated), `scans/latest`, `scans/history`,
`scans/{id}`, `cves/active`, `sbom`, `stack`, `compliance/status`, `baseline`.

WordPress: `wp/audit/latest`, `wp/audit/{scan_id}`, `wp/cron`.

.NET: `dotnet/target-frameworks`, `dotnet/efcore`.

Attack surface: `surface/latest`, `surface/{id}`.

## Workflows

**Bootstrap** → `detect_stack` → `init_project` → `install_toolchain` →
`security_scan_full` (or `scan_wordpress`) → `set_baseline`.

**Before PR** → `review_pr` → `triage_findings` → `prioritize_findings` →
`suggest_fix`.

**Periodic audit** → `audit_executive` (stack-aware) → `risk_score` →
`compliance_evidence`.

**WP-only** → `scan_wordpress` + `wp_audit` + `wp_cron_audit` +
`wp_rest_audit` → `wp_recommend_hardening`.

**.NET-only** → `dotnet_target_framework_check` + `scan_dotnet_secrets` +
`scan_sast` + `dotnet_efcore_audit` → `dotnet_describe_setup`.

**Active DAST** → `map_attack_surface` → `scan_dast` (target app must
already be running; `scan_dast` never starts, builds or stops it).

**Reachability** → `map_attack_surface` → `validate_finding` (needs a prior
surface snapshot; validates whichever scan completed most recently — run
right after a SAST scan, not after `scan_dast`).

## Anti-patterns

- Don't run `security_scan_full` for tiny changes — use `review_pr`.
- Don't `suppress_finding` before `triage_findings`.
- Don't propose fixes without `suggest_fix` (you'll lack source context).
- Don't shell out to scanners directly — you lose persistence + baselines.
- Don't run `scan_wordpress` on a non-WP project — `detect_stack` first.
- Don't run `wp_audit` without WP-CLI — `install_toolchain tools=["wp-cli"]`.
- Don't run `scan_dast` before `map_attack_surface` — it refuses with
  `no_surface_snapshot` and has no route inventory to probe.
- Don't read a clean `scan_dast` result as "no injection vulnerabilities" —
  the own engine sends no injection payloads at all.
- Don't treat `validate_finding`'s `unreachable` as safety proof or use it to
  auto-suppress — it's an over-approximating import-graph signal, unavailable
  for Ruby/Java/C#/PHP, blind to dynamic imports.
